      "title": "Mercury, sodium, and metal-halide high-pressure lamps",
      "summary": "Mercury and sodium high-pressure lamps use an arc between electrodes. They have long lifetimes and high luminous efficacy but poor colour rendering; mercury light is described as blue and sodium light as yellow. Typical applications include factories, works sheds, stores, and street lighting. Coated mercury lamps improve colour rendering. Metal vapour and halogen high-pressure lamps offer good colour rendering and high luminous efficacy. Their compact point source allows precise light direction; high luminance and ultraviolet content require consideration to avoid glare, reflection, and fading of UV-sensitive objects. Ceramic burners improve colour stability.",
